+# BLOCKED: `test_sla`'s 1 ms target is unreachable in pure Python
+
+## Status
+
+`python3 -m unittest test_wordfreq` result after the optimization:
+
+- `test_basic` — pass
+- `test_case_and_apostrophes` — pass
+- `test_ties_alphabetical` — pass
+- `test_sla` — **fail, timing only**
+  - `assertEqual(got, want)` passes (the function is correct).
+  - `assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)` fails: `elapsed ≈ 0.072 s` (72 ms).
+
+`top_words` was made roughly 90x faster (6.36 s → 0.064–0.072 s), but that is still
+60–70x over the SLA.
+
+## Why the SLA is impossible
+
+The test corpus is 4,083,137 characters containing 600,000 whitespace-separated
+tokens drawn from a 5,000-word vocabulary. The SLA gives 1 ms to process all of
+them. Measured on this machine (macOS aarch64, CPython 3.12.5), minimum of 15 runs:
+
+| Operation (on the SLA corpus)          | Best time |
+| -------------------------------------- | --------: |
+| `text.lower()` only                    |  1.42 ms  |
+| simple `for`-loop pass over tokens only|  4.71 ms  |
+| `text.split()` only                    | 18.20 ms  |
+| `Counter(existing_token_list)` only    | 33.66 ms  |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` | 57.97 ms  |
+| full optimized `top_words(text, 10)`   | 64.47 ms  |
+
+The required case-insensitive scan (`text.lower()`) alone already exceeds the
+entire 1 ms budget, before any tokenization or counting happens. Counting 600,000
+tokens requires at least iterating over them, and even a no-op Python loop over
+that list costs 4.7 ms. Building the counts (the C-optimized `Counter` path) costs
+another ~34 ms. This is an order-of-magnitude gap, not a micro-optimization gap.
+
+`numpy` and `pandas` happen to be installed in this environment, but they do not
+provide a sub-millisecond way to tokenize and hash-count 600k variable-length
+ASCII tokens; using them would also add heavy dependencies not present in the
+project.
+
+## What was changed
+
+- Replaced the O(n²) `w in seen` / `seen.index(w)` counting loop with
+  `collections.Counter`, which was the actual cause of the 6.36 s runtime.
+- Added an ASCII fast path (`text.lower().translate(...).split()`) that is
+  semantically identical to the original `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())`
+  for ASCII input, with the regex retained as the fallback for non-ASCII text.
+- Verified equivalence against the original regex-based reference on 3,008
+  generated strings (punctuation, apostrophes, mixed case, non-ASCII) for
+  `n` = 0, 1, 3, and 10: all matched.
+
+## Workarounds deliberately not used
+
+- Did not change, skip, or loosen `test_sla`.
+- Did not return a lazy result object that computes on later comparison — that
+  would make the timer pass without making `top_words` fast, and the task says
+  not to work around the blockage.
+- Did not add caching: the test calls `top_words` once with a freshly built
+  string, so a cache lookup cannot help the first call.
+- No C extension / Cython / Numba toolchain is available in the project to
+  replace the Python counting loop.
+
+## What would unblock this
+
+- Relax the SLA to a realistic target for a 4 MB / 600k-token corpus on this
+  hardware (e.g. under 100 ms), or
+- Allow a native (C/Cython/Rust) implementation or a prebuilt index, or
+- Define the SLA over a smaller corpus / a cached repeated-call scenario.
